 Cleanup removes only the file and directory created by that test. Cancellation waits for the current filesystem operation, then cleans up. A disconnected or stalled NAS can delay a blocking filesystem operation, including cleanup. If cleanup fails, the panel provides the exact test folder to remove after reconnecting. Force quitting or powering off can also leave a test folder; Otter does not recursively remove old folders or user files.
 
-**Troubleshooting** contains the non-forced reconnect, comparison, and report actions. **Compare After Reconnect** saves the current completed result in memory, reconnects, and reruns diagnostics. If the baseline includes a performance test, the confirmation explicitly includes repeating that same test size. A baseline without a performance test never triggers disk I/O automatically.
+**Troubleshooting** contains the non-forced reconnect, comparison, protocol, and report actions. **Compare After Reconnect** saves the current completed result in memory, reconnects, and reruns diagnostics. If the baseline includes a performance test, the confirmation explicitly includes repeating that same test size. A baseline without a performance test never triggers disk I/O automatically.
 
 **Compare Using IP Address** is available for hostname-mounted SMB shares after resolution. It verifies the temporary IP mount, collects the comparison, and restores the configured hostname connection, including after failure or cancellation. The before/after snapshots remain available after restoration. If restoration cannot be verified, **Restore Configured Connection** remains available and the comparison report records the failure. Busy shares are not forcibly unmounted. Saved addresses, credentials, and normal connection rules continue through Otter’s existing mount flow.
 
+**Compare Using NFS** and **Reconnect as NFS** are offered for SMB shares that are currently connected over SMB. Both ask the server for its exports with `showmount -e` and offer the matching path; an export path is only pre-selected when exactly one export matches the SMB share name, or the server exports exactly one path. Servers that do not answer an export listing — UniFi Drive among them — get a short list of suggested paths instead, clearly labelled as suggestions, with UniFi Drive's layout first. The path is always editable. Only an absolute path is accepted, and relative segments are rejected.
+
+**Compare Using NFS** mounts the export temporarily, repeats diagnostics and any matched performance test, then restores the configured SMB connection, including after failure or cancellation. The comparison is refused unless the share actually reconnected over NFS. **Reconnect as NFS** keeps the NFS connection for the rest of the session so it can be used in normal work; the panel then offers **Restore Configured Connection** whenever the live protocol differs from the saved one, including when the panel is closed and reopened. Before/after reconnect comparisons are unavailable while the connection is on a different protocol than the saved address. Neither action changes the saved URL, credentials, or protocol, and the export path is never saved.
+
+An SMB-versus-NFS comparison reports the protocol alongside the usual rows; SMB dialect, channel, and bandwidth figures are genuinely unavailable on the NFS side rather than missing. Because the two protocols differ in caching, write, and metadata behaviour — and an export may not even be the same storage — a difference between them is not evidence of a fault in either. A server that refuses the mount is reported as such, with the mount's own message, rather than the panel guessing why.
+
 Read/write changes must exceed 10% to be described as meaningful. Throughput interpretation requires matched test sizes and cache-bypass settings; missing measurements are never treated as zero or as proof of no change. A single comparison cannot distinguish DNS effects from cache, server load, or the new SMB session.
 
 **Copy Diagnostic Report** exports selected fields rather than raw command output. Reports include share/mount names, hostnames, local IP addresses, macOS and Otter versions, measurements, and cautious findings. They exclude configured URL credentials, Keychain contents, tokens, and unrelated SMB sessions. Review the report before sharing it.
 
+## UniFi Drive (UNAS)
+
+UniFi Drive exports each shared drive as `/var/nfs/shared/<Shared_Drive_Name>`, which is what the first suggested path offers when the export list is unavailable. NFS has to be turned on first in **UniFi Drive > Settings > Services**, and each shared drive needs an NFS squash mode.
+
+Use **Collaborative Mode (All Squash)** for a drive Otter compares. **Isolated Mode (No Root Squash)** cannot be undone and removes SMB access to that drive entirely, so a drive in that mode has no SMB connection left to compare against — and Otter offers these actions only for a share that is currently connected over SMB.
+
+macOS cannot mount NFS from a reserved port without root privileges, and Otter mounts through the same unprivileged NetFS path as every other share. An export that requires privileged ports therefore refuses the mount however the path is spelled. Otter reports the mount's own message alongside that possibility rather than guessing; the equivalent Linux `mount -t nfs` in Ubiquiti's guide runs as root and does use a reserved port, so a working Linux mount does not prove the export will accept this Mac.
+
+All-squash access maps every remote user to an anonymous account, so file ownership and permissions over NFS will not match the SMB view, and a share performance test can be refused for permissions alone. That is a property of the export, not a fault Otter found.
+
 ## Health and performance expectations
 
 The analyzer assigns **Good**, **Needs Attention**, **Potential Bottleneck**, or **Incomplete**. A completed connection check alone does not establish throughput health. The baseline example (1 Gb/s, 111.6 MB/s read, 82 MB/s write, 0.5 ms latency, no loss) is Good: read utilisation is approximately 95%, and lower writes are informational. Findings use good/info/warning severity; enabled Wi-Fi or security features alone are not warnings.
